# .dockerignore
#
# Controls what gets sent to the Docker daemon as the "build context" before
# the Dockerfile even runs. Keeping this list tight makes `docker build`
# faster (less to upload/hash) and stops secrets or dev-only junk from ever
# being copyable into an image layer by an accidental `COPY . .`.

# --- Version control ---
# The Dockerfile doesn't need git history or git's own config to build the app.
.git
.gitignore

# --- Secrets ---
# Never let real credentials end up inside an image layer - anyone who pulls
# the image (or a layer cache) could extract them. Env vars belong at
# `docker run` time (e.g. `--env-file .env`), not baked into the build.
.env
.env.*
# ...except a placeholder/example file, which is safe to ship as documentation.
!.env.example

# --- Python build/runtime artifacts ---
# Regenerated automatically; shipping stale host-compiled bytecode risks
# version/platform mismatches with the image's own Python interpreter.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.ruff_cache/

# --- Local virtual environment ---
# This is rebuilt from scratch inside the image via `uv sync`. The host venv
# may contain binaries compiled for the host's OS/architecture (e.g. macOS),
# which would silently break on the Linux base image.
.venv/

# --- Dev-only content ---
# Notebook used for ad hoc prompt experimentation (see pyproject.toml's ruff
# `extend-exclude`) - not imported by the app, no reason to ship it.
notebooks/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# --- Editor / IDE metadata ---
# Local tooling config, irrelevant to the running container.
.vscode/
.idea/
.cursorignore

# --- OS cruft ---
.DS_Store

# --- Docker metadata itself ---
# Changing these shouldn't bust the build cache of earlier COPY layers, and
# the image has no use for its own build recipe at runtime.
Dockerfile
.dockerignore
docker-compose.yaml
